# The ledger of `metrics`: what the metrics libraries of Go, Rust and Python
# let a program do, one block per capability, and what Rebel answers. None of
# the three standard libraries keeps metrics, so the yardstick is the census
# the author chose on 2026-11-17, measured on this machine on 2026-11-18:
# Go's `github.com/prometheus/client_golang` 1.25.0 (`prometheus`,
# `promhttp`, `push`, `testutil`), `github.com/prometheus/common/expfmt`
# 0.74.0, OpenTelemetry Go's `go.opentelemetry.io/otel/metric`, `sdk/metric`
# and `exporters/otlp/otlpmetric/otlpmetrichttp` 1.47.0 and Datadog's
# `datadog-go/v5/statsd` 5.9.1 (`go doc -all`, the modules fetched into the
# scratch folder); Rust's `prometheus` 0.14.0, `metrics` 0.24,
# `metrics-exporter-prometheus` 0.17, `opentelemetry` and `opentelemetry_sdk`
# and `opentelemetry-otlp` 0.33, and `cadence` 1 (their rustdoc, built into
# the scratch folder); Python's `prometheus_client` 0.26.0,
# `opentelemetry-sdk` and `opentelemetry-exporter-otlp-proto-http` 1.45.2,
# `statsd` and `datadog` (`dir()`, installed into the scratch folder).
#
# Behaviour was asked of the three where they speak: the exposition of one
# registry by `client_golang` and by `prometheus_client`, byte for byte; the
# 0.0.4 format's edges by `expfmt.TextParser`, Prometheus's own parser (no
# `promtool` is on this machine, and `promtool check metrics` reads through
# the same parser); the lines of `datadog-go` and of Python's `datadog` and
# `statsd` received on a UDP socket; the Pushgateway's paths received by a
# server of the test; OTLP's bytes as `opentelemetry-proto` 1.45 serializes
# the same message. Where they disagree, the note says whose answer was taken
# and why.

capability registry/value  a registry the program holds and passes, no default and nothing global
  heroes  metrics.new_registry, metrics.Registry
  go      prometheus.NewRegistry, NewPedanticRegistry, Registry, Registerer, Gatherer, DefaultRegisterer, DefaultGatherer, promauto.With
  rust    prometheus::Registry, Registry::new, default_registry, metrics::set_global_recorder, with_local_recorder, set_default_local_recorder, Recorder, NoopRecorder, LocalRecorderGuard, metrics_exporter_prometheus::PrometheusBuilder, PrometheusRecorder, PrometheusHandle
  python  CollectorRegistry, REGISTRY, registry
  note    The language has no globals, so the defaults of Go (`DefaultRegisterer`, `promauto`), Rust (`default_registry`, the global recorder of `metrics`) and Python (`REGISTRY`) have no counterpart: the program's own registry is passed `@`

capability registry/register  a family described, registered, refused where its name is taken, and taken out
  heroes  metrics.register, metrics.unregister, metrics.desc_of, metrics.Desc, metrics.Kind, metrics.EXISTS
  go      prometheus.Register, MustRegister, Unregister, Registry.Register, Registry.MustRegister, Registry.Unregister, AlreadyRegisteredError, Desc, NewDesc, NewInvalidDesc, Opts, DescOpt, Registry.Describe, Collector.Describe
  rust    prometheus::register, unregister, Registry::register, Registry::unregister, core::Desc, Opts, metrics::describe_counter, describe_gauge, describe_histogram, Key, KeyName, Metadata, Unit
  python  CollectorRegistry.register, CollectorRegistry.unregister, Metric
  note    A name is refused `exists` where it, or one of the samples it writes (a histogram's `_bucket`, `_sum`, `_count`), is another family's, as Go's registry refuses the collision when it gathers; here at once

capability family/counter  a counter, with labels or none, that only goes up
  heroes  metrics.counter, metrics.inc, metrics.add
  go      prometheus.Counter, NewCounter, CounterOpts, CounterVec, NewCounterVec, CounterVecOpts, Counter.Inc, Counter.Add, CounterVec.WithLabelValues, CounterVec.With, CounterVec.GetMetricWith, CounterVec.GetMetricWithLabelValues, CounterVec.CurryWith, CounterVec.MustCurryWith, promauto.NewCounter, promauto.NewCounterVec, metric.Int64Counter, metric.Float64Counter
  rust    prometheus::Counter, CounterVec, IntCounter, IntCounterVec, register_counter, register_counter_vec, register_int_counter, register_int_counter_vec, register_counter_with_registry, register_counter_vec_with_registry, register_int_counter_with_registry, register_int_counter_vec_with_registry, core::GenericCounter, core::GenericCounterVec, metrics::counter, metrics::Counter, CounterFn, opentelemetry::metrics::Counter
  python  Counter, Counter.inc, Counter.labels, opentelemetry.metrics.Counter, Meter.create_counter
  note    A series is named by its labels' values in the family's order, Go's `WithLabelValues`; `With` by a map and `CurryWith` would be a second way. A negative amount or NaN is refused `invalid` (Go panics, Python raises); `+Inf` is taken, as Go takes it

capability family/gauge  a gauge set, raised and lowered, set to a moment
  heroes  metrics.gauge, metrics.set, metrics.inc, metrics.dec, metrics.add, metrics.set_to_time
  go      prometheus.Gauge, NewGauge, GaugeOpts, GaugeVec, NewGaugeVec, GaugeVecOpts, Gauge.Set, Gauge.Inc, Gauge.Dec, Gauge.Add, Gauge.Sub, Gauge.SetToCurrentTime, GaugeVec.WithLabelValues, GaugeVec.With, GaugeVec.CurryWith, GaugeVec.MustCurryWith, GaugeVec.GetMetricWith, GaugeVec.GetMetricWithLabelValues, metric.Float64Gauge, Int64Gauge, Float64UpDownCounter, Int64UpDownCounter
  rust    prometheus::Gauge, GaugeVec, IntGauge, IntGaugeVec, register_gauge, register_gauge_vec, register_int_gauge, register_int_gauge_vec, register_gauge_with_registry, register_gauge_vec_with_registry, register_int_gauge_with_registry, register_int_gauge_vec_with_registry, core::GenericGauge, core::GenericGaugeVec, PullingGauge, metrics::gauge, Gauge, GaugeFn, GaugeValue, opentelemetry::metrics::Gauge, UpDownCounter
  python  Gauge, Gauge.set, Gauge.inc, Gauge.dec, Gauge.set_to_current_time, Gauge.track_inprogress, Gauge.time, opentelemetry.metrics.UpDownCounter, Meter.create_gauge, Meter.create_up_down_counter
  note    Go's `Sub` is `add` with a negative amount; Python's `track_inprogress` is `inc` before and `dec` after, since no block runs around the program's code. `set_to_time` takes the moment, the program reading `time.now`

capability family/untyped  a value of no kind, `untyped` in 0.0.4 and `unknown` in OpenMetrics
  heroes  metrics.untyped
  go      prometheus.UntypedOpts, UntypedFunc, NewUntypedFunc, ValueType
  rust    prometheus::proto::Untyped
  python  -

capability family/histogram  a histogram of observations in buckets, with its sum and count
  heroes  metrics.histogram, metrics.observe, metrics.observe_duration, metrics.DEFAULT_BUCKETS
  go      prometheus.Histogram, NewHistogram, HistogramOpts, HistogramVec, NewHistogramVec, HistogramVecOpts, Observer, ObserverFunc, Observer.Observe, ObserverVec, HistogramVec.WithLabelValues, HistogramVec.With, HistogramVec.CurryWith, HistogramVec.MustCurryWith, HistogramVec.GetMetricWith, HistogramVec.GetMetricWithLabelValues, DefBuckets, Timer, NewTimer, Timer.ObserveDuration, metric.Float64Histogram, Int64Histogram, WithExplicitBucketBoundaries
  rust    prometheus::Histogram, HistogramOpts, HistogramVec, HistogramTimer, DEFAULT_BUCKETS, histogram_opts, register_histogram, register_histogram_vec, register_histogram_with_registry, register_histogram_vec_with_registry, metrics::histogram, Histogram, HistogramFn, opentelemetry::metrics::Histogram, HistogramBuilder
  python  Histogram, Histogram.observe, Histogram.time, Histogram.DEFAULT_BUCKETS, opentelemetry.metrics.Histogram, Meter.create_histogram
  note    The default buckets are Go's eleven, from 0.005 to 10; Python's has 0.075, 0.75 and 7.5 more. NaN is refused `invalid`: Go counts it in `+Inf` and Python in no bucket, and either makes the sum NaN. `observe_duration` is Go's `Timer` and Python's `time`, the program measuring with `time.mark`

capability family/buckets  buckets made: evenly spaced, growing by a factor, or growing over a range
  heroes  metrics.linear_buckets, metrics.exponential_buckets, metrics.exponential_buckets_range
  go      prometheus.LinearBuckets, ExponentialBuckets, ExponentialBucketsRange
  rust    prometheus::linear_buckets, exponential_buckets
  python  -
  note    Each bound is computed as Go computes it, bit for bit for the first two; over a range with C's `pow`, which is exact where Go's `math.Pow` is a few units off: 1 where Go writes 1.0000000000000007. A count under 1 or a width or factor out of range fails `invalid`, where Go panics

capability family/summary  a summary of observations with chosen quantiles, its sum and count
  heroes  metrics.summary, metrics.Quantile, metrics.quantile_of
  go      prometheus.Summary, NewSummary, SummaryOpts, SummaryVec, NewSummaryVec, SummaryVecOpts, SummaryVec.WithLabelValues, SummaryVec.With, SummaryVec.CurryWith, SummaryVec.MustCurryWith, SummaryVec.GetMetricWith, SummaryVec.GetMetricWithLabelValues
  rust    prometheus::proto::Summary, proto::Quantile
  python  Summary, Summary.observe, Summary.time
  note    The quantiles are estimated by the targeted algorithm of Cormode, Korn, Muthukrishnan and Srivastava (2005), written from the paper, each within its error, as Go's are; none writes only the sum and count, as Python's summary always does. The rank sought is the ceiling of `q * n`, so the median of 1, 2 and 3 is 2

capability family/summary-window  a summary's quantiles over a sliding window of age
  heroes  pending
  go      SummaryOpts.MaxAge, SummaryOpts.AgeBuckets, SummaryOpts.BufCap
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    Go forgets observations older than ten minutes by default, reading its clock at each observation. A registry is a value with no clock, so the window needs the moment of each observation given to `observe`, a second form of it; not written until it can be one form

capability family/info-stateset  facts as an info's labels, and the state of something among several
  heroes  metrics.info, metrics.set_info, metrics.state_set, metrics.set_state
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  Info, Info.info, Enum, Enum.state
  note    Python's own: written as gauges in 0.0.4 and as `info` and `stateset` in OpenMetrics, as Python writes them. A changed fact is a new series, which `remove` takes away; Python's `info` replaces the series of the same labels

capability family/labels  constant labels on every series, a unit, a name made of its parts
  heroes  metrics.with_const_labels, metrics.with_unit, metrics.full_name, metrics.Label
  go      Opts.ConstLabels, Labels, BuildFQName, WithUnit, WrapRegistererWith, WrapRegistererWithPrefix, WrapCollectorWith, WrapCollectorWithPrefix, ConstrainableLabels, ConstrainedLabels, UnconstrainedLabels, metric.WithUnit, metric.WithDescription
  rust    prometheus::Opts::const_labels, opts, labels, metrics::Label, IntoLabels, SharedString
  python  namespace, subsystem, unit and labelnames of each metric's constructor
  note    Go's wrapping of a registerer adds labels or a prefix to every family registered through it; here each description says its own, `with_const_labels` and `full_name`. A unit must end the name, as Python requires; Python adds it where it is missing

capability series/remove  a series taken out, every series of a family taken out
  heroes  metrics.remove, metrics.remove_all
  go      MetricVec.DeleteLabelValues, MetricVec.Delete, MetricVec.Reset, CounterVec.DeleteLabelValues, CounterVec.Delete, CounterVec.Reset
  rust    MetricVec::remove_label_values, remove, reset
  python  Counter.remove, Counter.clear, Gauge.remove, Gauge.clear, Counter.reset
  note    Python's `reset` puts a counter back at zero: `remove` and the next `inc` do the same

capability series/remove-partial  every series whose labels hold some given values taken out
  heroes  metrics.remove_matching
  go      MetricVec.DeletePartialMatch
  rust    -
  python  remove_by_labels
  note    A constant label is not matched, as Go matches the labels a series is made with

capability series/read  a value read back from a registry or from gathered families
  heroes  metrics.value_of, metrics.count_of, metrics.sum_of, metrics.sample_value
  go      testutil.ToFloat64, testutil.CollectAndCount, testutil.GatherAndCount, testutil.CollectAndCompare, testutil.GatherAndCompare, testutil.ScrapeAndCompare, testutil.TransactionalGatherAndCompare, testutil.CollectAndFormat, testutil.GatherAndFormat
  rust    -
  python  CollectorRegistry.get_sample_value, CollectorRegistry.collect, CollectorRegistry.restricted_registry
  note    A comparison with an expected text is `format_text(gather(r)) == text`, or `parse_text` and `sample_value` where only some samples matter

capability exposition/gather  every family of a registry with its samples, in Go's order
  heroes  metrics.gather, metrics.Family, metrics.Sample, metrics.Timestamp
  go      Registry.Gather, Registry.MustGather, Gatherer, GathererFunc, Gatherers, TransactionalGatherer, ToTransactionalGatherer, MultiTRegistry, NewMultiTRegistry, Metric, NewConstMetric, MustNewConstMetric, NewConstHistogram, MustNewConstHistogram, NewConstSummary, MustNewConstSummary, NewMetricWithTimestamp, NewInvalidMetric, Collector, CollectorFunc, DescribeByCollect
  rust    prometheus::gather, Registry::gather, proto::MetricFamily, proto::Metric, proto::LabelPair, proto::Counter, proto::Gauge, proto::Histogram, proto::Bucket, proto::MetricType, core::Collector, core::Metric
  python  CollectorRegistry.collect, Metric, Sample, metrics_core, samples
  note    Families by name and series by their labels' values, labels by name with `le` and `quantile` last, as Go's registry; Python keeps the order of registration and of labels. A constant metric of Go's is a `Family` built directly and written beside `gather`'s, the two lists joined by `lists.concat`

capability exposition/text  Prometheus's text format 0.0.4 written, as Go's client writes it
  heroes  metrics.format_text, metrics.CONTENT_TYPE_TEXT
  go      expfmt.MetricFamilyToText, expfmt.NewEncoder, expfmt.Encoder, expfmt.Format, expfmt.NewFormat, expfmt.FormatType, expfmt.Closer
  rust    prometheus::TextEncoder, Encoder, TEXT_FORMAT
  python  generate_latest, CONTENT_TYPE_PLAIN_0_0_4, exposition.floatToGoString
  note    Held byte for byte to `client_golang` 1.25 on counters, gauges of every edge of a float, histograms with and without labels, and summaries. Go writes `1` where Python writes `1.0`, and `_sum` before `_count` where Python writes them the other way; Prometheus reads both

capability exposition/openmetrics  the OpenMetrics text format 1.0 written
  heroes  metrics.format_openmetrics, metrics.CONTENT_TYPE_OPENMETRICS
  go      expfmt.MetricFamilyToOpenMetrics, expfmt.FinalizeOpenMetrics, expfmt.NewOpenMetricsFormat, expfmt.DefaultOpenMetricsAcceptedFormats, expfmt.EncoderOption
  rust    -
  python  openmetrics.exposition.generate_latest, CONTENT_TYPE_LATEST, CONTENT_TYPE_PLAIN_1_0_0
  note    Held byte for byte to Go's on the same registry, and to Python's on info and state sets. No `_created` line is written, as Go writes none unless asked (`WithCreatedLines`) and Python writes one unless told not (`disable_created_metrics`): a registry has no clock to read when a series begins

capability exposition/openmetrics-2  OpenMetrics 2.0 and the formats that escape UTF-8 names
  heroes  pending
  go      expfmt.MetricFamilyToOpenMetrics20, Format.ToEscapingScheme, Format.WithEscapingScheme, expfmt.ExtractSamples, expfmt.DecodeOptions
  rust    -
  python  openmetrics.exposition.CONTENT_TYPE_LATEST_2_0, escape_metric_name, escape_label_name, ALLOWUTF8, UNDERSCORES, DOTS, VALUES
  note    Names here are the classic `[a-zA-Z_:][a-zA-Z0-9_:]*`, which every Prometheus reads; names of any UTF-8 and their escapings are not written yet

capability exposition/parse  the 0.0.4 text format read
  heroes  metrics.parse_text
  go      expfmt.TextParser, expfmt.NewTextParser, TextParser.TextToMetricFamilies, expfmt.NewDecoder, expfmt.Decoder, expfmt.SampleDecoder, expfmt.ParseError
  rust    -
  python  parser.text_string_to_metric_families, parser.text_fd_to_metric_families, parser.parse_labels
  note    Go's `TextParser` was asked 67 texts, and the tests hold its verdicts on their edges, taken and refused alike: a family's samples apart are joined, a family with no sample is left out, `\t` refused as an escape, a carriage return and a trailing space refused. Samples are kept flat, as Python answers them

