capability exposition/negotiate  the format a scraper's `Accept` asks for
  heroes  metrics.respond
  go      expfmt.Negotiate, expfmt.NegotiateIncludingOpenMetrics, expfmt.NegotiateAccept, expfmt.ResponseFormat, expfmt.DefaultAcceptedFormats
  rust    -
  python  exposition.choose_encoder, exposition.gzip_accepted
  note    OpenMetrics where `Accept` weighs it above the 0.0.4 format, as Prometheus's own `Accept` does and Python answers; Go answers the 0.0.4 format unless the handler is built with `EnableOpenMetrics`

capability serving/handler  the answer to a scrape of `/metrics`, compressed where accepted
  heroes  metrics.respond
  go      promhttp.Handler, promhttp.HandlerFor, promhttp.HandlerForTransactional, promhttp.HandlerOpts, promhttp.HandlerErrorHandling, promhttp.Compression, promhttp.InstrumentMetricHandler, promhttp.Logger
  rust    metrics_exporter_prometheus::PrometheusHandle::render
  python  MetricsHandler, make_wsgi_app, make_asgi_app, start_http_server, start_wsgi_server, exposition.default_handler, asgi
  note    The server is `http`'s: a program's own loop answers `/metrics` with the registry it records into (`http.next_request` and `http.write_answer`), or its router (`web`). `start_http_server` in a thread would serve a copy the program no longer changes, the language sharing no value between threads

capability serving/instrument  a server's requests counted and timed by status and method
  heroes  metrics.register_http, metrics.observe_request, metrics.HTTP_REQUESTS, metrics.HTTP_DURATION
  go      promhttp.InstrumentHandlerCounter, promhttp.InstrumentHandlerDuration, promhttp.InstrumentHandlerInFlight, promhttp.InstrumentHandlerRequestSize, promhttp.InstrumentHandlerResponseSize, promhttp.InstrumentHandlerTimeToWriteHeader, promhttp.Option, promhttp.WithExtraMethods, promhttp.WithLabelFromCtx, promhttp.WithLabelFromRequest, promhttp.LabelValueFromCtx, promhttp.LabelValueFromRequest
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    A method is written in lower case where RFC 9110 names it and `unknown` otherwise, so a client cannot make series without end, as Go's handlers write it. Requests in flight and sizes are the program's own gauge and histogram: no handler is wrapped, the language having no closures

capability serving/client-instrument  a client's requests counted and timed
  heroes  refused: no closure wraps `http.send`, so the program records around its own call with `inc` and `observe_duration`, which is all Go's wrapper does
  go      promhttp.InstrumentRoundTripperCounter, promhttp.InstrumentRoundTripperDuration, promhttp.InstrumentRoundTripperInFlight, promhttp.InstrumentRoundTripperTrace, promhttp.InstrumentTrace, promhttp.RoundTripperFunc
  rust    -
  python  -

capability pushgateway  families pushed to a Pushgateway by group, added, and deleted
  heroes  metrics.push_to_gateway, metrics.push_add_to_gateway, metrics.delete_from_gateway, metrics.gateway_url
  go      push.New, push.Pusher, Pusher.Push, Pusher.PushContext, Pusher.Add, Pusher.AddContext, Pusher.Delete, Pusher.Grouping, Pusher.Collector, Pusher.Gatherer, Pusher.Client, Pusher.BasicAuth, Pusher.Header, Pusher.Format, Pusher.Error, push.HTTPDoer
  rust    -
  python  push_to_gateway, pushadd_to_gateway, delete_from_gateway, exposition.basic_auth_handler, exposition.tls_auth_handler, exposition.passthrough_redirect_handler
  note    A value holding more than letters, digits and `-._~` is written in base 64, an empty one `=`, as Python writes them; Go writes a space as `+`, which the Pushgateway reads as a plus. The body is the 0.0.4 format, Python's; Go pushes protobuf. Any 2xx is success; Go's `Delete` wants 202 alone. Authentication and headers are the `http.Client`'s and the request's

