  note    Each line is one datagram ended by a line feed, as Go's client writes it; Etsy's StatsD reads tags as part of the name, so a program for it gives none

capability statsd/count  a count sent, raised or lowered
  heroes  metrics.send_count
  go      statsd.Client.Count, Client.Incr, Client.Decr
  rust    cadence::Counted, CountedExt, Counter, ext::ToCounterValue
  python  statsd.StatsClient.incr, decr, DogStatsd.increment, DogStatsd.decrement, DogStatsd.count
  note    Go's `Decr` sends -1, Python's `decrement(2)` -2: a negative `value` does both

capability statsd/gauge  a gauge's value sent, or a change to it as Etsy's StatsD reads `+3`
  heroes  metrics.send_gauge, metrics.send_gauge_change
  go      statsd.Client.Gauge
  rust    cadence::Gauged, Gauge, ext::ToGaugeValue
  python  statsd.StatsClient.gauge, DogStatsd.gauge
  note    A value is written with the fewest digits and no exponent, Go's `21.5` and `-2`, where Python's `datadog` writes `-2.0` and `1e+21`. A negative value is sent as it is, which DogStatsD reads as the value; Python's `statsd` sends `0|g` before it, since Etsy's reads a sign as a change

